WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:23.089
Are you surprised to see him

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:25.191
openly criticizing DeSantis this early?

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:26.659
What do you think

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:28.895
No, I'm not surprised.

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:30.930
If you think back on the 16 primary,

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:32.232
he was taking shots

00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:33.233
at everybody in the race,

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:35.402
even people who were at zero and 1%

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:37.437
in the case of DeSantis.

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:38.104
There are a lot of polls

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:39.839
where DeSantis is ahead of Donald Trump,

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:41.408
including in key early states.

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:43.009
So I'm not surprised.

00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:44.077
I expect him to come after

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:45.578
DeSantis pretty hard.

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:46.913
You know, Trump's the only person

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:48.181
officially in the race right

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:50.083
now, but it sure looks like the status

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:50.683
this is going to run.

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:52.285
And if you look at the whole field,

00:00:52.285 --> 00:00:53.386
the Florida governor

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:54.120
looks like the one

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.523
who might have the reservoir of support

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:58.291
among all corners of the party

00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.793
that actually beat Donald Trump

00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:00.894
in a primary.

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:01.261
Well, then,

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:02.328
what do you make about this

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:02.762
phone call

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:03.730
we're learning about with Nikki

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.432
Haley calling him, saying she

00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:06.766
she wants to get in the race

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.368
and failing him out.

00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:09.569
And he said basically,

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:11.137
if that's what you feel in your heart,

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:11.871
go for it.

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:13.306
I mean, on one hand,

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:15.041
it really works to his advantage,

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:16.576
right, to have as many join the

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:17.610
race as possible.

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:19.946
Yeah.

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:20.880
A big field

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.282
who actually stays in it

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:23.750
through the voting helps

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.385
Donald Trump in 2016.

00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:27.220
He only got about 45%

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.221
of the Republican vote,

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.789
but he won the nomination

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.324
because of the winner take all system.

00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:32.659
You would just have to get the most votes

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:34.260
in these states to get all the delegates.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.162
And so if he could get a big field

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:38.331
that stays in it for far too long

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:39.833
it would really advantage him

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:40.333
or frankly,

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:42.836
anyone who kind of carries a strong base.

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:45.205
But it's not a majority of the party.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.773
My anticipation is

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:48.274
the field will be smaller

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:49.476
this time around.

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:50.610
And people will

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.712
drop out earlier than they did last time.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:53.813
What you really need,

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:54.481
if you want to take out

00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:55.081
Donald Trump,

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:55.748
is to get it down

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:57.717
to a one on one match with somebody

00:01:57.717 --> 00:01:59.252
that people really like.

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:00.019
And right now

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:00.987
it looks like the Sanders

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:01.488
is that first

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:02.655
and other people will give it a go.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:04.290
But certainly DeSantis

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:04.724
is in a

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:05.425
is in a class

00:02:05.425 --> 00:02:07.560
by himself of the non-Trump candidates.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:07.894
Yeah.

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:08.628
And on that note,

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:09.262
I mean, we've been seeing

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:10.463
some interesting polling data

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:12.398
out of those key early primary states.

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:13.833
Ron DeSantis coming in

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:14.734
well ahead of Trump

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:16.603
among Republicans in New Hampshire,

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:18.404
leading the former president by a full

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:19.472
12 points.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:20.974
There are similar numbers there in South

00:02:20.974 --> 00:02:21.508
Carolina is

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:22.375
I mean, with numbers

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:23.042
like that,

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:23.810
Scott, is Trump

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.211
losing his title as the party's

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:26.446
2024 frontrunner?

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:30.717
I think he is still

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:31.985
probably the frontrunner

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:33.253
if you take all the metrics

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:35.555
into account money organization

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:36.122
you know

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:37.123
a cadre of people

00:02:37.123 --> 00:02:39.392
who are fully dependent upon him

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:40.360
for their livelihoods.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:41.528
I mean he still is a quite

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:43.096
a formidable candidate.

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:44.697
So I guess nominally

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:45.632
he's the frontrunner.

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.133
And plus there are no other candidates

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.735
technically at the moment.

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:50.803
But you can't look at this polling

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:51.871
if you're on this

00:02:51.871 --> 00:02:53.206
and not be fully intrigued

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:54.174
by where you stand.

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:54.841
You've done nothing.

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:56.176
You've not filed a committee.

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:57.644
All you've done is run for reelection.

00:02:57.644 --> 00:02:59.812
And Trout, your Democrat opponent.

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:00.780
And yet you are

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.248
right here in the driver's

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:03.983
seat of the candidates

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:04.851
who want to take on

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:06.719
and maybe take out Donald Trump.

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.188
So I expect him to run,

00:03:08.188 --> 00:03:09.088
and I think he's going to

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:10.023
be quite popular.

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:11.524
I've seen a lot of polling, Pamela,

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:12.659
from around the country

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:14.460
in different states, private polling.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.795
I've got to tell you,

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:16.996
Trump still popular.

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:18.565
The Santas is just as popular,

00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:20.800
but he doesn't have nearly the amount

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.235
of detractors inside the party

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:23.303
that Trump does.

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:23.636
Yeah.

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:25.038
I mean, even just anecdotally

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:26.439
in our home state of Kentucky,

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:27.340
when I was talking to folks

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.942
there, recently,

00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:29.809
there was a

00:03:29.809 --> 00:03:30.510
theme of folks

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:31.878
who used to support Trump saying,

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:33.780
I'd like to see Ron DeSantis

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:34.614
get in this race.

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:36.449
I'd like to see him run.

00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:38.685
But, you know, at the same time,

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:41.588
you've got to look at the reality here.

00:03:41.588 --> 00:03:41.921
Right,

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:43.723
and what Trump did the last time

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:45.758
and what he is capable of doing again.

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:47.160
Matthew Continetti,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.127
has an interesting piece

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:49.996
in The Free Beacon where he warns

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:51.464
that the US political class

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:52.065
is behaving

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:53.366
alarmingly similar

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:54.500
to how they did in the run up

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:55.235
to 2016

00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:56.803
writing once again

00:03:56.803 --> 00:03:57.637
Trump's opponents

00:03:57.637 --> 00:03:59.439
think he will just disappear.

00:03:59.439 --> 00:04:00.273
No Republican

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:01.975
wants to attack him directly,

00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:03.676
but the only way to thwart him,

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:05.878
Trump is to make voters choose

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:07.213
someone else.

00:04:07.213 --> 00:04:09.249
I mean, at what point do his challengers

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:10.450
start challenging him?

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.685
Even Ron DeSantis has been,

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:14.821
you know,

00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:15.922
hasn't really been doing

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:16.322
that, doing

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:18.024
the kind of direct engagement

00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:19.692
you might expect.

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:21.828
I would say pretty soon

00:04:21.828 --> 00:04:22.395
you're going to see

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:24.864
people start to take further steps.

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:26.432
I mean, for the people who are not

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:27.800
Trump org is Santas.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:28.501
One of the problems

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:29.636
they have is figuring out,

00:04:29.636 --> 00:04:31.371
is there any oxygen for me?

00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:31.838
I mean,

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:32.705
Trump and DeSantis

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.907
take up nearly the entire

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:36.376
oxygen in the primary right now.

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:37.644
Everybody else is in the low

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:38.344
single digits.

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:39.012
Even former

00:04:39.012 --> 00:04:40.246
Vice President Pence, who's

00:04:40.246 --> 00:04:41.147
well known,

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:42.982
is a single digit candidate right now.

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:44.917
So I'm not sure it's as much Trump

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:45.585
keeping them out

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:46.753
as it is the Santas

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:48.921
because of how popular

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.223
nationally with the Republicans.

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:51.758
But I agree with Matthew.

00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:52.492
At some point,

00:04:52.492 --> 00:04:54.127
somebody is going to have to take him on

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:55.328
and somebody is going to have to make

00:04:55.328 --> 00:04:56.462
Republicans choose.

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:57.096
And I will say,

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:57.764
I think

00:04:57.764 --> 00:04:59.666
broadly across the American electorate,

00:04:59.666 --> 00:05:01.034
in both parties,

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:03.336
there is a demand for new candidates,

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:05.638
new blood, new generation of leadership.

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:08.241
And the party that chooses new

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:10.143
is going to have a huge advantage

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:11.577
if the other party chooses.

00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:13.379
Oh, and if I were Ron DeSantis

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:14.847
and that's something I'd be making

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:17.050
the case for, Democrats are going, Biden,

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:17.617
let's pick

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:19.285
somebody new and ride this thing

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:20.987
and win it and put together a coalition

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:22.622
that can carry the national popular vote.

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:23.856
All right.

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:25.058
Scott Jennings, nice to see you.

00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:25.758
Thank you.

